+// chimera: Clear() for a renderer that goes on running. Clear() frees every
+// vertex loader and every native vertex format, which is all a shutdown needs;
+// the GL rebuild after a savestate load (OGLGfx::ChimeraRebuildGLObjects) calls
+// it with the machine about to draw again, and three things still pointed at
+// what it freed:
+//
+//   - g_main_vertex_loaders / g_preprocess_vertex_loaders, the loader of each
+//     vertex format group, looked up again only when the group is marked dirty;
+//   - s_current_vtx_fmt, compared by address with the next loader's format.
+//
+// So the first primitive after a load ran a loader that had been freed. In a
+// scene with one vertex format the freed memory was still intact and nothing
+// showed; in a 3D scene it had been handed out again, the loader's stride and
+// its compiled code were whatever was there now, and the core died asking for
+// two and a half billion bytes of vertex buffer and then jumping into data
+// (Pro Rally 2002's demo race on a GTX 1060, on every load).
+//
+// The pointers are set to null as well as marked for refresh, on purpose: a
+// loader that is not looked up again is then a null dereference on the first
+// draw, in any scene, and not a read of freed memory that mostly works.
+void ChimeraClearAndForget()
+{
+  Clear();
+  g_main_vertex_loaders.fill(nullptr);
+  g_preprocess_vertex_loaders.fill(nullptr);
+  s_current_vtx_fmt = nullptr;
+  MarkAllDirty();
+}
